  "subtitle": "A live-television console whose defining control is one heavy metal lever the technical director physically shoves to push one camera off the air and bring another on — with no undo",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Grass Valley Group 1600 Series production switcher (vision mixer) is the museum's cleanest embodied-performance artifact: hardware from the company that, by 1968, had set the industry standard for production switchers. Its defining control is the transition lever — the T-bar — a heavy physical fader the technical director (TD) drives by hand to dissolve, wipe, or cut between two live video buses while the show is literally going out on air. There is no undo: mid-transition the TD's hand is in the signal path, and because the program bus is always 'hot,' the lever is a momentary act rather than a state toggle — exactly why it reads as strange next to modern software panels. It fills a wholly unrepresented category: live-broadcast, irreversible performance hardware, distinct from the Sony BVE-600 edit controller (post-production, offline) already in the collection. It also carries an extraordinary pop-culture hook: a Grass Valley switcher console served as the Death Star superlaser controls in Star Wars (1977), and vintageTEK confirms the GVG 'T' handle of a model 1600 was the lever an actor throws to fire the weapon — Grass Valley's own engineers found it hilarious.",
